## Symptom

x-left has had **no relay connectivity for 55 days** and nothing noticed.

- Every `connector-state.json` under `~/.c2c/repos/*/broker` on x-left records
  `last_sync_ts == last_ok_ts == 1784814085` = **2026-07-23T13:41:25Z**, with
  `last_error_* = null` and `registered: []`.
- The host rebooted **2026-08-07** (`uptime -s`). Nothing restarted the connector.
- `c2c doctor` printed the following **1,175 times** across
  `~/.grok/sessions/**/terminal/*.log`:
  `state: registered_unreachable - prior connector sync state exists but the
   connector is down (stale last_sync - restart connect; ...)`
  plus ~700 `connector: down (last sync NN.Nd ago ...)` lines ranging from 25.8d
  to 54.8d.

## Root cause

The connector's `exit 3` is documented as "exiting so a supervisor can restart it
(B211/B228)". The only supervisor that exists is `C2c_relay_managed.supervise`,
i.e. the parent process created by `c2c start relay-connect`. There is:

- **no systemd unit on either host** — `systemctl --user list-unit-files | grep c2c`
  returns nothing on xsm and on x-left; `~/.config/systemd/user/` contains no c2c
  unit;
- **no boot hook** of any other kind.

So the supervision story terminates at the first reboot, OOM, or logout. On
x-left the supervisor itself died on 2026-07-23 (the instance log ends mid-normal-sync
with no shutdown line after 10 starts / 9 `connector exited 3` restarts) and the
machine has been relay-dark ever since.

## Aggravating factor: the documented fallback is unsupervised

`c2c doctor`'s own remediation string is:

```
c2c start relay-connect 2>/dev/null || c2c relay connect &
```

The fallback arm is a **background child of the calling agent's shell**. It dies
with the tool call / session that spawned it, which reproduces the exact failure
the hint is meant to fix. `C2c_relay_managed.unsupervised_warning` already warns
about this (B235) — the doctor should not be recommending it.

## Fix direction

- Ship a `systemd --user` unit for `relay-connect` (`Restart=always`,
  `StartLimitIntervalSec=0`, `WantedBy=default.target`) and install/enable it from
  `c2c install self`; add it to the uninstall manifest.
- On hosts without systemd, fall back to a documented supervisor rather than
  `&`.
- Stop emitting `c2c relay connect &` as a remediation anywhere; point at
  `c2c start relay-connect` only.
- Make prolonged connector-down an escalation rather than a line reprinted 1,175
  times: after N consecutive doctor runs reporting `registered_unreachable`,
  surface it as a blocking health failure.

## Related host issue

`c2c` is not on the PATH of a non-login shell on x-left (`ssh x-left c2c` ->
`command not found`), although `~/.local/bin/c2c` exists (0.15.1, built
2026-08-09). Any hook or supervisor invoked from a non-login shell there would
fail to find the binary. Worth confirming `c2c install` writes an absolute path
into everything it generates.

## Evidence

Full investigation: `.collab/findings/2026-09-17T01-50-00Z-kesa-quip-relay-connector-crashloop.md`

Lane: install-supervision, serial with B290 in one worktree (.worktrees/b296-b290-install) — both touch install surfaces. Decisions: unit ships under data/; installed+enabled by c2c relay enable and by c2c install self ONLY when Relay_activation resolves active (B300 — never auto-start on a local-only host); ExecStart uses the absolute c2c path; no remediation string anywhere recommends backgrounding with &; hosts without systemd skip gracefully; uninstall removes it.